        <div id="what-is-a-roof-rake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Is a Roof Rake? Two Very Different Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            A roof rake is a long-handled tool for pulling or pushing snow off a roof while you stand on the ground. It exists for one reason: to stop snow from sitting on the lower edge of the roof long enough to cause ice dams, leaks or a heavy load. The term also has a second meaning that confuses a lot of searches, so it is worth clearing up first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pull Rakes</h3>

          <p>
            A pull rake is a wide blade, usually 20 to 25 inches, on a telescoping pole. You reach up the roof, set the blade behind the snow and pull it down toward the eave. The Snow Joe RJ204M and Suncast adjustable rake work this way. They are light, cheap and simple, and they work in any snow, but you pull the snow down toward yourself and you lift the full weight of the tool on every stroke.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slide Rakes</h3>

          <p>
            A slide rake, like the Avalanche!, Snow Joe RJ208M, SNOWPEELER and ROOF-RAZOR, has a cutting frame with a long fabric or plastic slide attached. You push the frame up the roof and it cuts a strip of snow, which rides down the slide and drops off the eave. You never drag snow across the shingles, and you do much less lifting. The trade-off is a learning curve and more parts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Other Rake: the Rake Edge of a Roof</h3>

          <p>
            If you searched what a rake on a roof is, you may want the roofing term. The rake is the sloped edge of a gable roof, running from the eave up to the ridge, as opposed to the eave, which is the horizontal bottom edge. Rake trim or rake flashing covers that edge on shingle and metal roofs. This guide is about the snow tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Long Should a Roof Rake Be? Single-Story vs Two-Story
          </h2>

          <p>
            Reach is the first number to check, and the most commonly misread. A rake's pole has to cover the distance from your hands to the eave, plus the three to five feet of roof you want to clear, plus the distance you stand back from the house so falling snow does not land on you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Single-Story Homes</h3>

          <p>
            On a typical single-story house, a pole of about 15 to 21 feet clears the lower few feet of roof from a safe distance. The Avalanche! 500 and 750, at 15.5 feet, are sized for this; the 21-foot Snow Joe RJ204M and Suncast give more room to stand back.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two-Story Homes</h3>

          <p>
            A two-story eave sits roughly twice as high, and the pole runs at a steeper angle, so you need much more length. Look at 25 feet and up: the ROOF-RAZOR at 25, the Snow Joe RJ208M at up to 28, and the SNOWPEELER at 30. Suncast says its 21-foot rake handles most two-story homes; measure yours before relying on it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Measure Before You Buy</h3>

          <p>
            Measure from the ground to the eave, add the slope distance you want to clear, and remember you will stand several feet out from the wall. A rake that only just reaches forces you to work right under the eave, which is exactly where snow and ice fall.
          </p>
        </div>

