        <div id="shingles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does a Roof Rake Damage Shingles? Only If It Scrapes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A roof rake can damage shingles, and the damage usually comes from the way it is used rather than the tool itself. Asphalt shingles get brittle in the cold, and a blade dragged hard across them can scrape off the protective granules, crack tabs or catch an edge and lift it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rollers, Wheels and Glide Pads</h3>

          <p>
            The best protection is a head that never touches the roof. The Snow Joe RJ208M has 3-inch wheels, the Avalanche! 500 has 1.5-inch wheels for asphalt shingles, the Avalanche! 750 has 3-inch wheels for tile, shake, metal and solar, the ROOF-RAZOR has wide wheels, and the SNOWPEELER has glide pads.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blade Material</h3>

          <p>
            Poly and graphite blades, like the Snow Joe RJ204M's and the Suncast's, are gentler than bare metal if they do touch. Metal blades cut packed snow better but need more care.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leave an Inch or Two</h3>

          <p>
            Do not rake down to bare shingles. Leaving an inch or two of snow protects the roof surface and still removes most of the weight and the meltwater source for ice dams.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Roof Rake Safety: Ground, Power Lines and Falling Snow
          </h2>

          <p>
            Roof raking injures people every winter, almost always in predictable ways. These rules are not optional, and they come before any spec in this guide. Follow the maker's instructions for your rake as well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rake From the Ground, Never a Ladder or the Roof</h3>

          <p>
            A roof rake is designed to be used from the ground. Never climb a ladder to use one, and never get onto a snowy roof. Ladders slide on snow and ice, and a long pole levered from a ladder throws you off balance. If you cannot reach from the ground, hire a professional.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stay Away From Power Lines</h3>

          <p>
            Look up before you extend the pole. Aluminum poles conduct electricity, and contact with an overhead line or the service drop to your house can kill you. Fiberglass conducts much less when clean and dry, but wet or dirty fiberglass can still conduct, so no pole is safe near a line. Keep the whole tool well clear, and leave any roof near a line to a professional.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch for Falling Snow and Ice</h3>

          <p>
            Snow comes off a roof in slabs, and icicles and chunks of ice come with it. Stand back from the eave, wear a hat and eye protection, keep children and pets away, and never rake above a doorway someone may walk through.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Chip at Ice Dams</h3>

          <p>
            Never hack at an ice dam with a rake, shovel, hammer or ice pick. You will damage shingles, gutters and flashing and may tear the roof open. Clear the snow above the dam and call a professional with steam equipment if the dam is causing leaks.
          </p>
        </div>

