        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Use a Roof Rake, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Good technique clears more snow with less effort and keeps the roof and you in one piece.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Check Overhead and Plan Where Snow Will Land</h3>

          <p>
            Before you assemble anything, look for power lines and the service drop, and decide where the snow will fall. Keep it off walkways, vents, air conditioners, gas meters and shrubs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Start Short and Work Up</h3>

          <p>
            Begin at the eave with the pole at its shortest useful length. Clear a strip a couple of feet deep, then extend and work further up the roof. Pulling a full load from high on the roof at full extension is hard on your back and the pole.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Work in Strips, Not Scoops</h3>

          <p>
            With a pull rake, take bites of a few inches of depth at a time and pull straight down the slope. With a slide rake, push the frame up into the snow and let each strip run down the slide before you take the next.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Stop at the Right Height</h3>

          <p>
            Clear the lower three to five feet of roof, which is where ice dams form, and leave an inch or two of snow on the shingles. You do not need to clear the whole roof.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-rake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When to Rake Snow Off a Roof (and When Not To)
          </h2>

          <p>
            You do not need to rake after every dusting. Raking is worth the effort in a few situations.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">After Heavy Snow</h3>

          <p>
            Rake after a storm leaves roughly six inches or more, especially when more is forecast. Fresh, light snow is far easier to move than snow that has settled, thawed and refrozen.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Ice Dams Have Formed Before</h3>

          <p>
            If your house has a history of ice dams or icicles along the gutters, rake after most significant snowfalls. Removing the snow above the eave removes the meltwater that feeds the dam.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When the Load Is Heavy</h3>

          <p>
            Wet snow and ice weigh far more than powder. Signs of an overloaded roof include sagging, cracking sounds and interior doors that start to stick. If you see those, get out and call a professional or your local building official rather than raking.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Not to Rake</h3>

          <p>
            Skip raking during freezing rain, in high winds, at night or whenever you cannot see the roof edge and the lines above it clearly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ice-dams" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Does Roof Raking Prevent Ice Dams? It Helps, With Limits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ice dams form when heat escaping from the house melts snow on the upper roof. The water runs down to the cold eave, refreezes and builds a ridge of ice that traps more water behind it, which can back up under shingles and into walls and ceilings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Raking Does</h3>

          <p>
            Raking the lower few feet of roof removes the snow that would melt and feed the dam. It is one of the most effective things a homeowner can do on the day, which is why so many rakes market it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Raking Cannot Fix</h3>

          <p>
            Raking treats the symptom. The cause is warm air and heat reaching the roof deck. Air sealing the attic floor, adding insulation and keeping soffit and ridge vents clear are the long-term fixes, and a roofer or energy auditor can tell you which your house needs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clear Gutters Before Winter</h3>

          <p>
            Gutters packed with leaves freeze solid and give an ice dam a head start. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-gutters-before-winter/">cleaning gutters before winter</a> covers it, and <a href="/best-gutter-guards/">gutter guards</a> keep them clear.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="metal-roofs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. The Best Roof Rake for Metal Roofs and Solar Panels
          </h2>

          <p>
            Metal roofs shed snow on their own, often suddenly and all at once. That makes the area below dangerous and changes what a rake is for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protect the Finish</h3>

          <p>
            A blade dragged across painted steel can scratch the coating and invite rust. Choose a rake that rides on wheels high enough to clear the seams, like the Avalanche! 750 with its 3-inch wheels, which Avalanche! lists for metal roofs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Expect Sudden Slides</h3>

          <p>
            Raking a metal roof can trigger the whole sheet of snow to release. Stand well back, use the longest pole that keeps you in control and never rake above an entry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solar Panels</h3>

          <p>
            Glass and frames scratch easily, and most panel makers have their own cleaning rules. Avalanche! lists the 750 for solar panels; check your panel or installer's guidance before raking them at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-raking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. After Raking: Walkways, Driveways and Ice
          </h2>

          <p>
            Snow raked off the roof has to go somewhere, and it usually lands on the paths you need to use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clear the Landing Zone</h3>

          <p>
            Raked roof snow is dense and heavy. A good shovel, like those in our <a href="/best-snow-shovel/">best snow shovel guide</a>, or a <a href="/best-snow-blower/">snow blower</a> for large amounts, clears it before it freezes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Treat Refreeze</h3>

          <p>
            Meltwater from roofs and icicles refreezes on steps and walks overnight. Use an ice melt that is safe for your surface; our <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">ice melt for concrete</a> guide compares the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Rake Ready</h3>

          <p>
            Store the rake assembled or with its sections together near the door you will use, so it is ready the morning after the next storm. See our <a href="/snow-removal-tools/">snow removal tools</a> guide for the rest of the winter kit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Raking From a Ladder or the Roof</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A roof rake is a ground tool. Ladders slide on snow and a long pole levered from a ladder throws you off balance. If you cannot reach from the ground, hire a professional.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Extending the Pole Without Looking Up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Aluminum poles conduct electricity, and fiberglass can when wet. Check for overhead lines and the service drop before every extension and keep well clear.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Scraping Down to Bare Shingles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dragging a blade across cold shingles strips granules and cracks tabs. Leave an inch or two of snow and choose a rake with wheels or rollers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Chipping at Ice Dams</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hacking at ice with a rake, hammer or pick damages shingles, gutters and flashing. Clear the snow above the dam and call a professional with steam equipment for the ice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Standing Under the Eave</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Snow, icicles and ice fall in heavy slabs. Buy enough reach to stand well back, and keep people and pets out of the landing zone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best roof rake?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the Snow Joe RJ208M is the best roof rake: Snow Joe lists up to 28 feet of reach, a 20-foot debris slide and 3-inch wheels that keep the head off the shingles. Choose the Avalanche! 500 for a fiberglass pole on a single-story asphalt roof, or the Avalanche! 750 for metal, tile and solar panels.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a roof rake damage shingles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can if the blade scrapes. Cold asphalt shingles are brittle, and a blade dragged hard across them can strip granules or crack tabs. Choose a rake with wheels, rollers or glide pads, rake from the ground with light pressure, and leave an inch or two of snow on the roof instead of clearing to bare shingles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you use a roof rake?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check overhead for power lines first. Stand on the ground well back from the eave, start with a short pole and clear the lowest strip, then extend and work up. Pull straight down the slope with a pull rake, or push a slide rake up into the snow, and clear the lower three to five feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should you rake snow off your roof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rake after storms that leave roughly six inches or more, especially if more is forecast or your house has had ice dams before. Rake while the snow is fresh and light, before it settles and refreezes. Skip it during freezing rain, high winds or darkness, and call a professional if the roof sags or creaks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does roof raking prevent ice dams?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It helps. Clearing the lower few feet of roof removes the snow that melts and refreezes at the eave. It does not fix the cause, which is heat escaping into the attic. Air sealing the attic floor, adding insulation and keeping ventilation clear are the long-term fixes, along with clean gutters before winter. A roofer or energy auditor can tell you which your house needs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long of a roof rake do I need for a two-story house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually 25 feet or more, because the eave is higher and you must stand back from the wall. The ROOF-RAZOR lists 25 feet, the Snow Joe RJ208M up to 28 and the SNOWPEELER 30. Measure from the ground to the eave and add the slope you want to clear before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a roof rake near power lines?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Aluminum poles conduct electricity, and even fiberglass can conduct when wet or dirty. Look up before extending the pole, keep the whole tool well clear of overhead lines and the service drop to your house, and hire a professional for any part of the roof near a line. Call your utility if snow or ice is weighing on the line itself.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

